In a hot loop, objects are created in two places:

```js
const a = { x: 1, y: 2 };
const b = { y: 2, x: 1 };
```

Both are passed to `function len(p) { return Math.hypot(p.x, p.y); }`. How does V8 see these objects?

A breadth-first search uses an array as its queue:

```js
while (queue.length) {
  const node = queue.shift();
  for (const next of node.children) queue.push(next);
}
```

On a graph with 1 million nodes it is far slower than expected. Why?

A developer benchmarks a helper like this and reports that it takes about 0.001 ms per call:

```js
const t = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 1e6; i++) slugify('Hello World');
console.log(performance.now() - t);
```

In production the function is clearly much slower. What is the most likely flaw in the benchmark?

In V8, what is the elements kind of `arr` at the end?

```js
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(1.5);
arr.push('four');
```

Consider this code in V8:

```js
class Point { constructor(x, y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } }
const pts = data.map((d) => {
  const p = new Point(d.x, d.y);
  if (d.label) p.label = d.label; // about half the points
  return p;
});
const norm = (p) => Math.sqrt(p.x * p.x + p.y * p.y);
```

What state do the inline caches for `p.x` and `p.y` in `norm` end up in, and what is the best fix?

This code leaks about 1 MB per second, even though `theThing` is replaced on every tick:

```js
let theThing = null;
function replaceThing() {
  const originalThing = theThing;
  const unused = function () { if (originalThing) console.log('hi'); };
  theThing = {
    longStr: new Array(1e6).join('*'),
    someMethod() { console.log('m'); },
  };
}
setInterval(replaceThing, 1000);
```

Why does it leak in V8?

After this runs on the main thread, what is `buf.byteLength`? Type a number.

```js
const buf = new ArrayBuffer(1024 * 1024);
worker.postMessage({ frame: buf }, [buf]);
console.log(buf.byteLength);
```
